Regions I, II and III are bounded by squares. The perimeter of region I is 12 units and the perimeter of region II is 24 units. What is the ratio of the area of region I to the area of region III? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(9,0)--(9,9)--(0,9)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((9,0)--(15,0)--(15,6)--(9,6)--(9,0)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((9,6)--(12,6)--(12,9)--(9,9)--cycle,linewidth(2));
label("III",(4.5,4.5));
label("II",(12,3));
label("I",(10.5,7.5));
[/asy]

An acute isosceles triangle, $ABC$, is inscribed in a circle.  Through $B$ and $C$, tangents to the circle are drawn, meeting at point $D$.  If $\angle ABC = \angle ACB = 2 \angle D$ and $\angle BAC = k \pi$ in radians, then find $k$.

[asy]
import graph;

unitsize(2 cm);

pair O, A, B, C, D;

O = (0,0);
A = dir(90);
B = dir(-30);
C = dir(210);
D = extension(B, B + rotate(90)*(B), C, C + rotate(90)*(C));

draw(Circle(O,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--D--C);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, SW);
label("$D$", D, S);
[/asy]

$ABCDEFGH$ shown below is a cube with volume 1.  Find the volume of pyramid $ABCH$.

[asy]
import three;
triple A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H;
A = (0,0,0);
B = (1,0,0);
C = (1,1,0);
D= (0,1,0);
EE = (0,0,1);
F = B+EE;
G = C + EE;
H = D + EE;
